## Investigation findings — offline reproduction completed

**Bottom line:** protocol/transport failures are surfaced as tool errors by the installed DSH host, but a Composio action failure nested inside a valid MCP result is preserved as ordinary result data. Superagent has no deterministic per-action consumer that turns that payload into a failed ticket. This is a handling/observability boundary, not evidence that an external action succeeded or that errors are discarded.

### 1. What this repository actually wires

Inspected revision: `c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac`; the source files cited below had no working-tree differences during this investigation.

- [The Composio skill](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/skills/dsh-superagent-composio/SKILL.md#L10-L41) documents an externally configured `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client` profile plugin and search/schema/execute/connection workflows. No Composio-specific execution implementation exists under `src`; the repository's bundle patch does not itself configure a Composio MCP server. Authentication/connection ownership stays with Composio and its supported host configuration; no new credential manager is needed.
- [Worker tool restrictions](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/src/app/sessions.ts#L55-L62) and [workOnly](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/src/app/sessions.ts#L283-L296) do not allow inherited `mcp__composio__*` tools. Availability in an interactive host does not prove availability in managed ticket workers. I did not weaken that allowlist or inspect private profile/credential files.
- [DshExecutor.install](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/src/executor/dsh.ts#L476-L511) handles worker status, inbox claims, orchestrator user-message admissions, and `agent/error`; it does not classify `tool/result`. The [transcript projection](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/src/app/context.ts#L28-L32) retains tool-result content, but is not a failure policy.

### 2. Installed host boundary and reproduced results

Inspected and executed the actual installed `@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools` modules, both reporting version `0.2.1-rc.3`. These are external host packages, **not code owned by this repository**; observations apply to the installed builds, not an assumed pristine published release.

The MCP adapter's `createExecutor` validates `CallToolResult` and throws on `isError === true`. `ToolRuntime.dispatchToolBody` catches exceptions and produces an error result. It does not interpret application-specific `successful` fields in text JSON or `structuredContent`.

Using the real adapter + real ToolRuntime with a mocked provider callback:

| Synthetic provider outcome | ToolRuntime `isError` | Provider calls |
| --- | --- | --- |
| MCP `isError: true` | `true` | 1 |
| Thrown timeout after dispatch | `true` | 1 |
| Thrown HTTP 429 | `true` | 1 |
| Invalid MCP result shape | `true` | 1 |
| Text JSON `{successful:false, error:...}` | `false` | 1 |
| `structuredContent: {successful:false, error:...}` | `false` | 1 |
| Mixed batch: one successful action, one failed action | `false` | 1 |

**7/7 characterization assertions passed.** Both successful and failed batch entries were preserved. No automatic replay occurred in this adapter/runtime test. This does not establish what an LLM might retry on a later step, nor prove how every real Composio failure is encoded.

Minimal offline illustration (run with `DSH_ROOT` pointing to the installed DSH package; no credentials or network calls):

```js
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { resolve } from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
const require = createRequire(resolve(process.env.DSH_ROOT, 'package.json'));
const load = name => import(pathToFileURL(require.resolve(name)).href);
const { Context } = await load('@deepseek-ai/cordis');
const { ToolRuntime } = await load('@deepseek-ai/dsh-tools');
const { createMcpToolDefinition } = await load('@deepseek-ai/dsh-mcp-client');
const ctx = new Context();
ctx.provide('systemPrompt', { tools: () => () => {} });
const runtime = new ToolRuntime(ctx);
for (const protocolError of [false, true]) {
  let calls = 0;
  const name = 'mcp__composio__MOCK_ACTION';
  const unregister = runtime.register(createMcpToolDefinition(ctx, {
    name, rawName: 'MOCK_ACTION', description: 'Offline fixture',
    inputSchema: { type: 'object', properties: {} },
    call: async () => {
      calls++;
      return {
        isError: protocolError,
        content: [{ type: 'text', text: JSON.stringify({
          successful: false, error: 'MOCK action failed'
        }) }]
      };
    }
  }));
  const result = await runtime.execute({ name, callId: String(protocolError),
    arguments: {}, signal: new AbortController().signal });
  console.log({ protocolError, toolRuntimeIsError: result.isError, calls });
  unregister();
}
// false => toolRuntimeIsError:false, calls:1
// true  => toolRuntimeIsError:true,  calls:1
```

For exact installed-build provenance, SHA-256 of `lib/index.js`:
- MCP client: `2cd15b207d57bd0e21e5e088378969ca60c71010a36749bfd773f8f91de93dac`
- Tools runtime: `1e53932e7c4213ea24a7d87f06960b7a7702cb21f8a5741c97242b4a470a442a`

### 3. Superagent-level reproduction and existing retry behavior

Using the repository's existing mocked `startRuntime` harness, emitted two synthetic `session/event` / `tool/result` variants: an MCP-error result and an ordinary result containing `{successful:false}`. In both cases, after `runtime.idle()`, ticket state and delivery counts were unchanged and no failure report was created. Emitting a non-transient `agent/error` afterward did create the expected failed report. **2/2 tests passed.** This tests the repository's event handling, not live host escalation or direct worker access to Composio.

Also ran the existing [transient provider-failure regression](https://github.com/gera3d/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/c169620bced7cbd5fc42a8724f0fd45e2ef78cac/tests/runtime-concurrency.spec.ts#L191-L208): **1 passed, 55 skipped** with the name filter. It proves three retries for transient **agent-level** errors before reporting failure on the fourth occurrence. It is not a per-Composio-action retry policy.

Local-only evidence artifacts (ignored, not committed): `.artifacts/composio-failure-repro.mjs`, `.artifacts/composio-runtime-repro.spec.ts`, and `.artifacts/composio-repro.vitest.config.ts`. Executed:

```text
node .artifacts/composio-failure-repro.mjs <DSH-install-root>
pnpm exec vitest run --config .artifacts/composio-repro.vitest.config.ts
pnpm exec vitest run tests/runtime-concurrency.spec.ts -t 'lets a worker go on after a passing provider failure'
```

### Recommended next scope

1. First identify the intended caller: interactive host agent versus restricted ticket worker. Do not enable MCP tools broadly as an incidental fix.
2. At that caller's Composio consumption boundary, explicitly distinguish transport/MCP failure, application failure, mixed success, and uncertain completion. Preserve successful batch entries and diagnostics; do not treat top-level envelope success as all-actions success or blanket-retry mutating actions.
3. Add durable regression coverage and a clear recovery/reporting policy there, following Composio connection guidance. Sanitization, retry-after handling, and production correlation metadata were not established by these synthetic tests.

No production code, credentials, profiles, worker restrictions, or external app data were changed. GitHub commenting is the only external write in this investigation; no live failing Composio action was invoked.
